hi I'm Anthony Fisher with Reason TV and  we're here with Jamie Joyce the author  of moonshine a cultural history of  America's infamous liquor Jamie thanks  so much for joining us for having me  a lot of people think of moonshine is  like Prohibition era bathtub liquor and  now it's popular with the Brooklyn  hipsters so what exactly is moving  moonshine is a clear on aged whiskey  generally it's made from corn although  it's been used but people will use  anything that ferments you know  historically use just about anything but  generally it's been corn  it's also been barley it's also been rye  so a grain that's steeped on aged a lot  of times people will call moonshine  whiskey without the wood or bourbon  without the barrel where did moonshine  originate and it's always kind of been  associated with law breaking can you  like separate some of the myths from the  reality yeah moonshining has a long  history in the United States it came  with settlers from from Europe you know  the Ulster region a lot of these yes so  you had a lot of scots-irish coming to  this country bringing these traditions  with them and many of them were settled  in western Pennsylvania and so you had a  real movement going on there where and  we can't really call it moonshining at  the time because it was legal to make  this whiskey but it was this was before  a time than it was taxed so when the  government in 1791 put the impose the  distilled spirits tax then we started to  get people who were saying hey wait a  second this is not cool what do you try  to do to be talking about the colonial  eras and you know a big part of the  protests against the British is that the  new Americans thought they wouldn't have  to deal with this kind of stuff anymore  can you talk a little bit about the  whiskey rebellion yeah the whiskey  rebellion I think it came about because  suddenly we've just had the  Revolutionary War and then you had the  secretary of the Treasury saying hey we  Alexander Hamilton saying we need to  finance we need to pay for the  Revolutionary War debt in order to do  that we're going to impose a tax on  distilled spirits  this was something that people were very  uncomfortable with this was something  where we had just fought a war of  independence against unfair taxes and  and so suddenly to have the United  States government trying to impose this  tax people felt this was very unfair and  so there were organized protests largely  in western Pennsylvania and this is  where the Whiskey Rebellion grew out of  and so you had groups of what they they  were being called insurgents by the  federal government yes the name stick  around in different context so these  insurgents were going after tax  collectors and it became a violent  rebellion ultimately in which you had  President Washington sent out nearly  13,000 troops to quell this rebellion  and he himself led that charge out to  Peston Pennsylvania moving forward a  little prohibition ended in the 30s but  then there was more prohibition of  moonshine itself can you talk a little  bit about that  you know after prohibition a lot of  times people think well suddenly the  nation could drink again well that's not  entirely true there were still many  places throughout this country and there  is still places today that are dry so in  some states we even have dry counties  wet counties and within a dry County wet  cities you know so there's this real  holdover or hangover perhaps from  Prohibition so still you had you had  these communities where this was making  this clear on aged whiskey liquor this  was also part of cultural heritage this  wasn't just a way to make money although  it was a very good way for many people  to make money  it was very prominent in Appalachia  absolutely very prominent in Appalachia  but because liquor was still expensive  because jobs were scarce we're leading  into the Great Depression so there was  still people who needed to make money  and they relied on this family tradition  of making moonshine so this was a big  economic driver for a lot of people they  kept families afloat they kept put food  on the table you talked a little bit  about the struggles for me I was trying  to go legitimate yes well in back in you  know and we're talking in the  eighteen hundred's the mid to late-1800s  there wasn't really a movement for four  moonshiners to go legitimate per se but  they were trying to continue their  efforts to make their liquor without  being detected by the law and so it was  a real cat-and-mouse game between  revenuers and moonshiners and revenuers  were the revenue agents and these were  federal agents that came into play and  the believe it was 1861 where we had our  first income tax and liquor was taxed  and if you weren't paying that taxed you  could be arrested and so when you look  back at some of the very old the  original IRS tax records I mean every  year the IRS put out yearly bulletins  and about their activities these read  like Western novels because you have  stories of revenue agents riding on  horseback up into these hills and  valleys sniffing out the corn mash in  creeks you know really using all of  their senses and it was violent I mean  there were definitely people within  these small communities who really they  didn't feel like these moonshiners were  doing anything wrong can you talk a  little bit about how moonshining led to  the rise of NASCAR which is apparently  America's most popular spectator sport  all of you had all these people in the  south who were coming up as whiskey  tripperz that's what they called the  drivers so they were some of the most  skilled drivers around and so it was  just natural that they would become  involved in NASCAR one of the people  that I write about in my book moonshine  is a man that many people NASCAR fans  would know jr. Johnson and jr. Johnson  was one of the first inductees in the  NASCAR Hall of Fame now jr. Johnson as a  young man starting at about age 14  helped his father with his moonshine  whiskey business  so he was one of these guys who would go  out at 14 years old pre driver's license  but he knew how to drive and he would  deliver the liquor to people so he knew  how to drive fast and skillfully because  the law was after him all the time why  is moonshine culturally relevant now  because we have so many people  interested in this do-it-yourself  movement sort of back to these original  food  and in drink in this country and so it's  in order to make something you know to  make this liquor on your own is really  exciting to a lot of people it's under  the radar it's still against the law  remains against the law to make  distilled spirits even the wine and beer  you can you can make legally the title  of the book refers to moonshine as  America's most infamous liquor is there  a push for exporting moonshine to the  rest of the world is there an interest  in it well as we you know moonshine has  a long history in the United States and  because of that I think it has this  mystique that I think is appealing to  people in other countries and in fact I  do know that the folks at Kings County  distillery in Brooklyn which is the  distillery near where I live in Brooklyn  they're starting to export some of their  moonshine to Japan and so there's a real  interest in American culture and this is  certainly a deep deep part of American  culture thanks so much for talking to us  Jamie Joyce the author of moonshine a  cultural history of America's infamous  liquor for recent TV i'm anthony fisher
 

